WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:03.400
All right.

2
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.800
And next up we have Doctor Bridget,

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:10.280
kids from Oakland University
with once Upon an algorithm.

4
00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:13.960
The book authors
AI folk theories in the standardization

5
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.000
of creative practice.

6
00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:23.080
Hello.

7
00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:27.440
In his 2017 book Once Upon an Algorithm,
Martin Herwig introduces

8
00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:29.560
general readers to basic concepts

9
00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:33.440
and computing using analogies of common
fables and fairy tales.

10
00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:37.280
In doing so, Erwin's
interest is in making computer science

11
00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:41.000
intelligible outside its discipline
and in showing how skills like problem

12
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.080
solving and concepts
like algorithms are not just specific

13
00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:47.760
to software engineering and coding,
but rather part of an everyday life.

14
00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:50.960
I've used the same title for this project,

15
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.400
which takes a similar approach
of looking at the stories that we tell

16
00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.720
about algorithms
and artificial intelligence,

17
00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:00.920
the commonly shared folk theories
that have evolved in recent years

18
00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:04.680
as AI users and skeptics grapple
with what they know and don't know

19
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:07.360
about the tools
that are circulating right now.

20
00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:10.920
And this is part of a larger project
focusing on the romance industry,

21
00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:13.960
some a bit of an outlier here
because I'm setting a commercial genre,

22
00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.120
but it's a heavily book driven industry

23
00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:19.560
with lots of self-publishing,
and it's a digitized space

24
00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:23.600
where communities are pretty much
operating entirely digitally and socially.

25
00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:26.600
In the first stage of this project,
I conducted an internet based

26
00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:27.560
survey of readers

27
00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:31.360
about their attitudes toward generative
AI at different stages of writing.

28
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.080
Their attitudes toward authors uses
right from pre writing,

29
00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:38.840
like outlining and brainstorming,
to actually generating text,

30
00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:42.040
to turning completed manuscripts
into translations

31
00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:45.040
like we just talked about
or creating audiobooks.

32
00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:48.680
In addition, I've joined numerous writers
groups on social media where authors

33
00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:52.760
discuss the specifics of how they use
various tools, mostly Claude,

34
00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:56.600
and how they interact with the outputs
of those tools, if at all,

35
00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:01.200
how their incomes have been augmented
by faster output and automation.

36
00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:05.680
I've also just started the process of
demoing some AI writing tools for writers.

37
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.160
I'm really interested in the way that they

38
00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:11.000
they're like

39
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.320
mostly custom GPT with cool interfaces,
but they market themselves

40
00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:17.680
as being proprietary
and as doing something different from what

41
00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:20.680
you could get using
Claude or ChatGPT on your own.

42
00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:23.480
That's kind of beyond the scope of today's
talk, though.

43
00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:25.000
I'm also currently in the process

44
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:28.920
of conducting interviews
with romance authors from around the world

45
00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:32.000
to find out how they're using AI,
and if they're not, why not?

46
00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:35.840
So through all this research,
I've observed several common

47
00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:40.880
folk theories or informal beliefs
about how AI tools work based on

48
00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:43.680
user experiences and

49
00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:46.680
traded gossip,
rather than deep technical knowledge.

50
00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:51.640
Folk theories are mental representations
that humans use to structure experience,

51
00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:57.000
and they're often full of inconsistencies
gaps in idiosyncratic quirks.

52
00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:00.680
These folk theories are circulated
around social networks, and they become

53
00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:04.600
community wisdom that shapes
how writers use and expect other writers

54
00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:09.280
to use AI tools, and how writing workshops
and AI interfaces

55
00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:14.640
promise to help authors, often
selling these theories as best practices.

56
00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:16.320
These folk theories also shape

57
00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:20.080
how readers engage
or disengage with particular ebooks.

58
00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:25.000
The idea of an algorithmic folk
theory has been taken up in media studies,

59
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:28.840
digital humanities,
studies of human computer interaction HCI

60
00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.040
for more than ten years,
with various studies focusing on the way

61
00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:35.840
people explain and understand
recommendation algorithms,

62
00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:39.360
newsfeed on social media,
content moderation, etc.

63
00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:42.400
more recently,
some of us have been applying this concept

64
00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:45.680
to the way we see
people attempting to explain AI tools.

65
00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:47.800
I'm far from the first person
to make this connection

66
00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:50.840
between algorithmic folk theory
and AI folk theory.

67
00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:54.120
HCI studies have recently begun
to pay more attention

68
00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:57.120
to the e-book ecosystem
and to self-publishing

69
00:03:57.640 --> 00:04:01.280
some people, even specifically focusing
on the romance industry like me.

70
00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:05.480
But no one is yet focused on the idea
of how these AI folk theories

71
00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:09.120
specifically shape the culture of writing
and publishing ebooks

72
00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:12.040
and what impact that has on the market.

73
00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:14.920
So extending the concept
of the algorithmic folk theory,

74
00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:18.200
or as Tanya Boucher
calls it, the algorithmic imaginary into

75
00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:22.360
AI only seemed logical after I kept seeing
authors trade advice with each other

76
00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:26.360
about how to finesse prompts for Claude,
why Claude is better than Gemini

77
00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:29.920
or ChatGPT, or explain to each other,
often incorrectly,

78
00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:32.920
how text generators work,
and seeing readers

79
00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:36.040
express their own concerns
about plagiarism and slop.

80
00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:39.880
This is very much research and progress,
so I welcome your ideas and your feedback.

81
00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:41.520
But here's where I am so far.

82
00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:44.520
I'll start with the most obvious
folk theory,

83
00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:48.000
which I'm sure everyone has encountered
on the reader's end.

84
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:51.400
It involves lists of AI isms
that are believed to be telltale

85
00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:54.520
signs that attacks was generated by AI
in academia.

86
00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:55.920
Two years ago,

87
00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:59.640
it was students use of the word delve
that professors decided was a sign.

88
00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:03.120
Their essays had been written
by ChatGPT previously.

89
00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:07.920
It was the use of em dashes, I mean, I,
I encountered several versions of this.

90
00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:13.240
One was that overuse of em
dashes was indicative of generated text.

91
00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:14.320
Another theory was

92
00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:17.840
it was substitution of hyphens for m
dashes and maybe vice versa.

93
00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.720
In 2025, Sam Altman admitted on a podcast
that OpenAI had added more frequent

94
00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:27.080
use of m dashes to ChatGPT
because they thought people liked them.

95
00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:29.680
But later that year, OpenAI announced
it was lessening

96
00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:32.480
the frequency of their use
after public complaints.

97
00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:34.280
So that one does appear
to be somewhat true.

98
00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:38.320
But of course, the use of M as a flair in
writing comes from classic literary texts

99
00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:43.080
that llms have been built upon, like here
we have Emily Dickinson and a

100
00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:46.240
line from White Noise with the word delve.

101
00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:46.600
Right?

102
00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:50.320
So, like all AI isms,
it's less a quirk of a chatbot

103
00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:53.280
and more reflective of the idiosyncrasies
of certain human writing,

104
00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:56.560
which may be amplified
with particular prompts or outputs.

105
00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:00.760
On writer's end, folk theories
abound about how to prompt your favorite

106
00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:01.600
AI writing tool.

107
00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:06.120
To avoid the incorporation of these
AI isms, writers encourage each other

108
00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:09.920
to upload these lists to their preferred
chat bot, to train it, not to use them,

109
00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:13.800
lest their work be readily identified
as AI generated by a reader

110
00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:16.960
who may then leave
negative reviews or report

111
00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:20.000
a book for takedown on Amazon.

112
00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:23.120
Some tools also feature lists of these

113
00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:25.880
AI isms, with the promise
that their particular tool,

114
00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:26.920
which usually involves

115
00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:30.840
a hefty subscription fee, is smarter
at avoiding them than free

116
00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:34.840
versions of ChatGPT novel crafter,
for instance, lists

117
00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:40.280
the words palpable, tangible and raced
as three primary examples of AI isms.

118
00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:43.280
But such lists are not scientific
or precise,

119
00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:47.320
and that this particular list has no date
attached to it is especially concerning,

120
00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:51.240
since we know most commonly used text
generators no longer

121
00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:53.600
operate on closed training sets.

122
00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:57.160
Writers desire to avoid AI isms
is deeply connected to the phenomenon of

123
00:06:57.160 --> 00:07:02.040
AI witch hunts, which I think are keynote
sort of, you know, danced around here.

124
00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:04.240
I've written about this elsewhere,
and it's kind of

125
00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:06.000
outside
the scope of our presentation today.

126
00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:09.840
But I do think it's worth noting that
sharing lists of words, phrases,

127
00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:14.600
punctuation that indicate text generation,
it's a major part of AI folk theory.

128
00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:17.680
Another folk theory views Claude

129
00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:20.120
as temperamental
and endows it with personality.

130
00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:25.560
So, Katherine, this feeds really nicely
off of your presentation theories.

131
00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:29.200
Explain how to prompt around these moods
and authors celebrate

132
00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:33.000
when Claude is cooperative and helpful
as a sign that they're good at using AI,

133
00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:34.360
or that Claude likes them.

134
00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:35.720
Personally,

135
00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:39.080
I don't think this one should come
as a surprise to those of us who study

136
00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:40.640
chatbots or voice assistants.

137
00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:44.480
Or we had a lovely panel
about Eliza yesterday.

138
00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:47.080
So anyone who's familiar
with the Eliza effect, right?

139
00:07:47.080 --> 00:07:50.040
We anthropomorphize technology,
which AI chatbots

140
00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:52.400
have been specifically designed for.

141
00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:54.640
They interact with us
like friends and colleagues.

142
00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:58.440
They're imbued with human names
and women's voices and friendly rejoinder.

143
00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:02.680
So it's only logical that when they don't
perform exactly as we expect,

144
00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:05.760
rather than seeing it as a problem
with our prompting, we view them

145
00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:08.760
as having personalities or moods
that might fluctuate.

146
00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:12.640
In prior studies of how users interact
with social media platforms, researchers

147
00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:16.400
similarly found that users would interpret
algorithms as emotional,

148
00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:17.840
or that the algorithms

149
00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:21.680
were interpreting users emotions
in their recommendation of content.

150
00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:25.000
So again, what's happening with
AI isn't necessarily different than what

151
00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:29.040
preceded it with different technology,
but nonetheless, it helps us understand

152
00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:32.480
why writers make the choices
they do when using AI tools.

153
00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:36.200
And it tells us where we can encourage
more AI literacy, which is the point.

154
00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:37.600
Michael Ridley is similarly made

155
00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:40.720
about algorithmic folk
theories and algorithmic literacy.

156
00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:44.120
In the interest of time,
I'm just going to leave it at those two,

157
00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:48.480
because ultimately, which specific
folk theories authors have come up with

158
00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:52.080
is less important than the impact
of trading this folk wisdom

159
00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:56.440
and full theories, the impact that it has
on digital writing and publishing.

160
00:08:56.520 --> 00:09:01.120
The circulation of lists of AI isms
has exacerbated a problem wherein readers

161
00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:03.840
feel overly
confident calling out what they perceive

162
00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:06.840
as AI generated text,
which can have an impact on a writer,

163
00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:10.480
sales and social status
within the community at the same time,

164
00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:13.520
because these lists tell writers
what to avoid or or what

165
00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:14.960
to edit out of their work,

166
00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:17.960
there's a standardization
of writing to meet perceived market

167
00:09:17.960 --> 00:09:21.040
demands,
and a common criticism of generated texts

168
00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:24.280
is that its outputs are standard
and bland and unoriginal.

169
00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:28.960
But writing to avoid being used of using
AI might also result in writing

170
00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:33.120
that is bland, standard, and unoriginal,
because potential creative flourishes

171
00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:36.120
might be perceived
as having been generated by a chat bot.

172
00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:39.840
When I began this research, it seemed like
the conclusion it was pointing to

173
00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:43.240
was that all these folk theories
that are presented as best practices

174
00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:46.760
are standardizing writing and publishing,
and I expected to argue

175
00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:50.560
that there's real shame in that,
a decline in creative possibilities,

176
00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:52.080
but that perhaps it was something

177
00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:55.800
understandable in a general like romance,
because it's a commercial genre.

178
00:09:56.200 --> 00:09:58.560
However,
as I've gotten deeper into this project,

179
00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:01.640
when I actually see happening
as a literary house of cards,

180
00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:03.560
authors are constantly trading tips,

181
00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:07.280
giving workshops to each other, writing
blog posts, and posting social media

182
00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:10.720
videos about what does and doesn't work
to attract and sell books,

183
00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:13.640
the best way to write, how to use Amazon
more effectively, etc.

184
00:10:13.640 --> 00:10:17.520
like all of this exists
separate from AOO or Nonuse,

185
00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:20.760
and there's a lot of culture
about how this there's a lot of research

186
00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:23.800
about how this culture of sharing creates
a community.

187
00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:27.280
What better school refers to as networked
reading, or what other scholars

188
00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:31.320
have referred to as the relational labor
of being an e-book author?

189
00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:35.440
Today, when I counter all these advice,
tips and posts, am often baffled at

190
00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:40.000
the lack of evidence being used to support
any particular theory or advice.

191
00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:44.440
But I seem to be an outlier in a community
where folk wisdom is taken for granted.

192
00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:47.720
So as authors have turned to AI assistants

193
00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:51.520
for assistance or for text generation,
they're trying to interact

194
00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:54.920
with a complex technology
and they swap advice to each other.

195
00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:58.920
Full theories about how best to prompt
why certain prompts do or don't work,

196
00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:01.360
what kind of filtering and safeguards
their favorite

197
00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:04.360
AI tools have in place,
and how to work around it.

198
00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:08.280
How to work around getting an output
that will be pinged as AI generated

199
00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:11.600
among those who refuse to AI,
those same theories circulate as ways to

200
00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:15.840
spot the telltale signs of an author's
use of AI, or as proof of why

201
00:11:15.880 --> 00:11:19.840
writing with AI isn't as good,
and so series that may or may not

202
00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:24.040
be rooted in technical truth
become accepted as objective reality.

203
00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:27.160
The existence of AI folk theories
shouldn't really surprise us.

204
00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:31.120
Rather, it's an extension of an industry
that has been built on a sharing culture

205
00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:35.920
in which peer to peer advice and
recommendations become community wisdom.

206
00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:36.720
Thanks.
